<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elizabeth of Pomerania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1347–<a href="/years/1393">1393</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-griffins">House of Griffins</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> was the daughter of Duke Bogislaw V of Pomerania and granddaughter of King Casimir III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. In 1363 she became the fourth wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> and was crowned Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and Holy Roman Empress. She was the mother of Emperor Sigismund, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and Bohemia, and was famed for her physical strength.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Elizabeth was the daughter of Bogislaw V, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, and Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, whose parents were King Casimir III of Poland and Aldona of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. She married Charles IV on 25 May 1363 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, a year after the death of his third wife, Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swidnica">Schweidnitz</a>. The bride was 16 and the groom 47. Charles married her mainly for a diplomatic reason: the match helped to break the anti-Czech coalition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iv-duke-of-austria">Rudolf IV, Duke of Austria</a>, in which Polish and Hungarian kings took part.</p><p>She was crowned Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 18 June 1363, and on 1 November 1368 Pope Urban V crowned her empress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elizabeth is reputed to have been vigorous, self-confident and physically strong. Her relationship with Charles is described as good and harmonious, and it has been portrayed in art, as in the play A Night at Karlstein. When Charles fell seriously ill in 1371, she walked on foot to the cathedral and offered gifts in prayer for his health. She does not seem to have wielded political influence. She was tormented by the fact that Charles preferred his children from his earlier marriages, and she could not change his mind.</p><p>Her children were Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-ii-of-england">Richard II of England</a>; Sigismund, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>; John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a>; Margaret, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-burgrave-of-nuremberg">John III, Burgrave of Nuremberg</a>; and two sons who died in infancy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Charles died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 29 November 1378, Elizabeth's stepson Wenceslaus IV, the son of Charles's previous wife, took the throne. She then cared for her own two surviving sons, above all the elder, Sigismund, whom she supported in his efforts to become king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. She outlived her husband by 15 years and died in Hradec Králové in 1393. She was buried beside him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral">St. Vitus Cathedral</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elizabeth of Pomerania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His dynasty kept close ties with Poland, and his daughter <b>Elizabeth</b> married Emperor Charles IV.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerania-stolp">Duchy of Pomerania-Stolp</a></li></ul></section>
